: abstract
  We prove co-RE-completeness, and thus undecidability, of the following
  problem: given a single (connected) polycube,
  decide whether it tiles 3D Euclidean space by translations.
  We reduce from Wang tiling using the decorated two-prime Sudoku construction of Greenfeld and Tao and a cyclic encoding adapted from OpenAI's 3D aperiodic tile, and apply a reduction of Kim to make it connected (via faces).
  Dimension three is optimal: translational monotiling is known to be
  decidable in $\Z^2$ and for a single (possibly disconnected) polyomino in $\R^2$.

: tcolorbox[colback=figblue!6!white,colframe=figblue!65!black,boxrule=0.6pt,arc=2pt,left=10pt,right=10pt,top=8pt,bottom=8pt]
  This paper gives a human-readable overview of the
  results, proof ideas, and formal theorem statements. The proof is verified
  in Lean. See @sec:approach for details of our approach, and
  [the repository](https://github.com/edemaine/translation-tiling-paper)
  [[TilingRepo]]
  for the complete Lean code and a longer LLM-written technical article.

# Introduction @=sec:intro

Given $k$ finite subsets of $\Z^d$ (***prototiles***),
can (infinitely many) copies of those subsets be *translated*
to cover all of $\Z^d$ without overlaps?
For which values of $k$ and $d$ is this problem decidable?
In this paper, we prove undecidability for $k=1$ and $d=3$.

The decidability question has a long history, summarized in @tab:related.
The first result is Golomb's implementation [[Golomb]]
of the undecidable Wang tiling problem [[Berger]],
which works in 2D but requires arbitrarily many prototiles.
In 2D, Ollinger [[Ollinger]] reduced the number of prototiles to a constant
(11), which was subsequently improved in a series of papers;
%Subsequent bounds were 10 and 9 by Yang [[YangTen,YangNine]],
%8 and 7 by Yang and Zhang [[YZEight,YZSeven]], and 5 by Kim [[KimPlane]],
%all with connected polyominoes.
%Allowing disconnected polyominoes gives bounds of 4 by Yang and Zhang
%[[YZFour]] and
the current record is 3 by the MIT--ULB CompGeom Group [[Trominoes]].
%(although this result requires one of the prototiles to be disconnected).
In 3D, the first undecidability result used 6 prototiles
[[YangSix]],
% to 3 by Yang and Zhang [[YangThree]] and independently
%the MIT--ULB CompGeom Group [[Trominoes]],
and the current record is 2 [[Kim]];
we improve to the minimum possible number of 3D prototiles, 1.
3D is also the minimum dimension for undecidability with 1 prototile,
given known algorithms for 2D [[Bhattacharya,GTStructure]].
%even if we allow disconnected polyominoes.

